# Nil Pointer & Safe Access in Go

Nil pointer dereference is one of the most common runtime errors in Go. Understanding how to prevent and handle it is an essential skill for every Go developer.

## Nil Pointer Value

```go
var p *int
fmt.Println(p == nil) // true
fmt.Println(p)        // <nil>
```

## Nil Pointer Dereference Panic

```go
var p *int
// fmt.Println(*p) // PANIC: nil pointer dereference!

if p != nil {
    fmt.Println(*p)
} else {
    fmt.Println("pointer is nil")
}
```

## Pointer to Interface Gotcha

```go
var a Animal    // nil interface
var d *Dog      // nil pointer
a = d           // a contains (*Dog)(nil)

fmt.Println(a == nil) // FALSE! interface has type info
fmt.Println(d == nil) // true

// Safe check:
if dog, ok := a.(*Dog); ok && dog != nil {
    fmt.Println(dog.Sound())
}
```

## Safe Patterns

```go
// Early return
func getUserName(u *User) string {
    if u == nil { return "unknown" }
    return u.Name
}

// Nil-safe method
func (n *Node) Length() int {
    if n == nil { return 0 }
    return 1 + n.Next.Length()
}

// Optional values via pointer
type Profile struct {
    Bio *string // nil = not set
}
func (p *Profile) GetBio() string {
    if p.Bio == nil { return "(no bio)" }
    return *p.Bio
}
```

